== The Child’s Duties to His Parents

=== §. 96. #emph[Of the meaning of the first verse of the sixth chapter.]

#smallcaps[From] the particular duties that concern #emph[husbands] and #emph[wives,] the apostle proceeds to lay down those that concern children and parents. As before he laid down wives’ duties before husbands’, so here he begins with children’s (who are inferior to their parents), and that for the same reasons that were given #footnote[§. 10.] before.

Besides, children are the fruits of the joining in marriage, and so they are fitly placed next to #emph[man] and #emph[wife.]

What concerns children is laid down in the sixth chapter of Ephesians, vv. 1, 2, 3. We will open the meaning of it point by point.

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 6:1.]

    #text(style: "italic")[Children, obey your#super[+] parents in the Lord, for this is right.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

The first word #emph[(children)] is in the original as fitting a word #footnote[τὰ τέκνα.] as could be used, for by its derivation it means those who are begotten and born. The other word #emph[(parents)] answers to it: it means those who beget and bring forth #footnote[οἱ γονεῖς. Who are to be counted children, and who parents.] children. Yet the words are not to be taken so strictly, as if none were meant but those who begot and brought forth, or those who were begotten and brought forth by them. For under the title #emph[parents] he includes all who are in the place of natural parents, such as #emph[grandfathers and grandmothers, stepfathers and stepmothers, fathers-in-law and mothers-in-law, foster-fathers and foster-mothers, guardians, tutors,] and other such governors. And under the title #emph[children] he includes #emph[grandchildren, stepchildren, sons-in-law and daughters-in-law, wards, pupils,] and the like. For an honor and a subjection is due from all who are in the place of children to all who are in the place of parents, though in a different kind, as we will #footnote[#emph[Treatise] 5, §. 56, 57, #emph[etc.] τὰ τέκνα.] show later. This word #emph[children,] which in the original is of the #emph[neuter gender,] also includes both sexes, #emph[males] and #emph[females, sons] and #emph[daughters.] So each of them is to apply the duties set out here to themselves as carefully as if both kinds had been named in particular.

He speaks of #emph[parents] in the plural number to show that he #footnote[τοῖς γονεῦσι.] means both sexes here too, #emph[father] and #emph[mother,] as the law names both. And he adds this relative word #emph[your.] By way #footnote[ὑμῶν.] of #emph[restraint,] it shows that not every child is bound to every parent. By way of #emph[extent,] it shows that whatever the condition of the parents may be, honorable or lowly, rich or poor, learned or unlearned, etc., their own children must not be ashamed of them, but must give them every duty they are bound to. If they are parents to children, they must be honored by children.

The word #emph[(obey),] under which all the duties of children are #footnote[ὑπακούετε.] included, means, by its Greek derivation, #emph[to listen with humble submission.] That is, to attend and give heed to the commands, reproofs, directions and exhortations given to them, and that with such reverent respect for the persons who give them that they make themselves conform to them.

It is a duty proper to inferiors, and it implies both #emph[reverence] and #emph[obedience.] The #footnote[ἀκούειν.] verb points to #emph[obedience;] the #footnote[ὑπό.] preposition, to #emph[reverence.]

Under this word #emph[(obey)] the apostle includes all #footnote[All duties of children included under #emph[obedience.]] the duties that Scripture everywhere requires of children. This is plain from his own illustration of it in the second verse, by the word #emph[honor,] which the law uses. So this word #emph[(obey)] is to be taken in as large an extent as that word #emph[(honor).]

#emph[Question.] Why is #emph[obedience] put for all the rest?

#emph[Answer.] 1. Because it is the hardest of all the rest, and the one children are most unwilling to perform. Those who yield to this willingly will balk at no duty.

2\. Because it is the surest evidence of the #emph[honor] a child owes his parent, and so of keeping the fifth commandment.

3\. Because children are bound to their parents. The duties they perform are not matters of courtesy, but of necessity. Their parents have power to command them and to demand them.

The added clause #emph[(in the Lord)] is in effect the same as #footnote[ἐν Κυρίῳ.] the one used #footnote[#emph[Chapter] 5, #emph[v.] 22.] before #emph[(as to the Lord).] It points to a #emph[limitation,] a #emph[direction] and an #emph[incentive.] It is a #emph[limitation,] showing that children’s obedience to their parents is to be kept within the obedience they owe to Christ, and may not go beyond its limits. It is a #emph[direction,] showing that in obeying their parents they must have an eye to Christ, and so obey them that Christ may approve of it. It is an #emph[incentive,] showing that parents bear the image of Christ, and in that respect children must obey their parents all the more.

The last clause of this verse #emph[(for this is right)] is an express #footnote[τοῦτο γάρ ἐστι δίκαιον.] reason to enforce the obedience just named. It is drawn from equity, and shows that this obedience agrees with all law; indeed, that it is due by way of #emph[repayment.] If children are not obedient to their parents, they do what is most unjust: they defraud their parents of their right.

The former phrase #emph[(in the Lord)] implies one reason; this one plainly points to another, as the first word #emph[(for)] declares.

=== §. 97. #emph[Of the meaning of the second verse.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 6:2.]

    #text(style: "italic")[Honor your father and mother (which is the first commandment with a promise),]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] very words of the #emph[fifth commandment] are cited here #footnote[Why the moral law is cited.] by the apostle to confirm the reason just given, that #emph[it is just and right to obey parents,] because God requires as much in the moral law. The law is more general than the apostle’s precept. For the law includes under it all the duties that every kind of inferior owes to his superiors, whether in the family, the church or the commonwealth; but the apostle’s precept is given to only one kind of inferior in the family. Yet the argument from a general to a particular is very sound and good, thus: #emph[All inferiors must] #footnote[A genere ad speciem.] #emph[honor their superiors; therefore children must honor their parents.]

By adding the express words of the law, the apostle shows that the subjection he required of children is no yoke he put on their necks on his own authority, but one the moral law has put on them. So this may be noted as a third reason, namely, God’s express charge in his moral law.

If I were to handle this law in its full extent, I would wander too far from the apostle’s purpose. I will therefore open it no further than it concerns the point in hand, that is, the duty of children.

#emph[Honor] includes here all the duties that children in #footnote[Honor includes all children’s duties.] any respect owe to their parents. It implies both #emph[an inward reverent esteem] and #emph[an outward dutiful submission.] Indeed, it also implies #emph[repayment] and #emph[maintenance.]

#emph[Honor] is used in relation to parents for two reasons especially.

1\. To show that parents bear God’s image. For #emph[honor] is properly due to God alone; it is due to the creature only as it stands in God’s place and carries his image.

2\. To show that it is an honor to parents to have dutiful children, just as it is a dishonor to them to have disobedient children.

Both #emph[father] and #emph[mother] are expressly named, to take away #footnote[Why both father and mother are named.] from children every pretense for neglecting either of them. For through the corruption of nature we are prone to look for many excuses to exempt ourselves from the duty we are bound to; and if not wholly, then in as large a part as we can. Some might think that if they honor their father, who is their mother’s head, they have done what the law requires. Others may think they have done as much if they honor their mother, who is the weaker vessel. But the law, naming #emph[father and mother,] condemns the one who neglects either of them. Yet the #emph[father] is set in the first place. This shows that if a conflict should arise between them, so that both could not be obeyed together, the father commanding what the mother forbids, the father is to be preferred (especially if it is not against the Lord).

The words that follow, #emph[(which is the first commandment] #footnote[How the fifth commandment is the first with a promise.] #emph[with a promise),] are fitly set in a #emph[parenthesis,] because they are not the words of the law. The apostle inserted them as a reason to enforce the law, and so they make a #emph[fourth reason.]

#emph[Question.] In what respect is this commandment called #emph[the first with a promise?]

#emph[Answer.] 1. The word the apostle uses here properly #footnote[ἐντολή.] means an affirmative precept, as our English word #emph[(commandment)] does. Now of the affirmative precepts, it is #emph[the first with a promise.]

2\. Scripture often applies the word law to the second table in particular, as where he says, “he who loves his neighbor has fulfilled the #footnote[Rom 13:8.] law,” and so in other places.

Now this is the first commandment of the second table.

3\. It is true in general of all the commandments: among the ten, it is the #emph[first with a promise.]

#emph[Objection.] The second commandment has a promise attached #footnote[The difference between the promise in the second and in the fifth commandment.] to it.

#emph[Answer.] 1. What is attached to the second commandment is not expressly a promise. It is rather a declaration of God’s justice in taking vengeance on transgressors, and of his mercy in rewarding those who keep the law. Yet I do not deny that a promise is implied by consequence; but here it is expressed.

2\. The promise implied there is only a general promise made to those who keep the whole law, and that is why he uses the plural number, #emph[commandments.] But here a particular promise is made to those who keep this commandment in particular.

2\. #emph[Question.] Why then is it called the #emph[first,] when no other commandments with a promise follow? #footnote[#emph[First] taken simply.]

#emph[Answer.] This word #emph[(first)] does not always refer to some other that follows. It is often taken simply, to show that none was before it. So the word #emph[firstborn] is used in the law, #footnote[Exod 13:2. Matt 1:25.] and so Christ is called the #emph[firstborn] son of #emph[Mary.]

The word #emph[promise] shows that this fourth reason includes some benefit that comes to the children themselves who honor their parents. The benefit is expressly named in the next verse, which we will consider point by point later.

=== §. 98. #emph[Of aiming at our own good in seeking the good of others.]

Here in general we may note that

#emph[It is not unlawful to aim at our own good and benefit in doing the duties to others that God requires at our hands.] For what God himself sets before us, we may seek and aim at. There are many similar promises in Scripture, and many approved prayers grounded on those promises, by which the truth of this doctrine is confirmed to us. #emph[Hezekiah] #footnote[Isa 38:2, 3.] makes the good service he had done to God and his church a ground for obtaining a longer life, and so do others.

For God lays no duty on any man without aiming in it at the good of the one who performs the duty, as well as of the one to whom it is performed. By this he would show that his commandments are no tight yokes and heavy burdens, but means of procuring the good of those who fulfill them.

How highly this commends the kind regard that #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.]] God has for all the sons of men, seeking their good in every place where he sets them, whether of authority or of subjection!

How this ought to stir us up to keep willingly and cheerfully #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.]] the laws God commands us, and to perform the services he requires of us, since by doing so we procure our own good!

How fully this may satisfy, and even stop the mouths of, #footnote[3\. #emph[Use.]] all who are discontented with their places and grumble against the subjection God requires of them!

What a good direction and resolution this may be to many #footnote[4\. #emph[Use.]] who are moved in conscience to seek the good of others, and yet doubt whether in doing so they may aim at their own good or not! To make this case clear by one instance, which may serve for many: a minister is faithful in his place and very hardworking, and in that respect has a good conscience, but he also has a tender and weak conscience. He doubts whether by his work he may seek a living for himself, fearing that in this he seeks himself, and not simply the building up of God’s church. But by the doctrine just stated we see that both may be aimed at: for God commands the one and promises the other. As we have one eye on God’s commandment for direction, so we may have the other on his promise for encouragement.

Yet because through the corruption of our nature we are too #footnote[Cautions in seeking our own good.] prone to seek ourselves, some cautions are to be carefully observed in this point.

1\. That we do not seek our own good by any #emph[transgression,] for it is promised to #emph[obedience.]

2\. That we do not seek ourselves and our own good so wholly that we neglect others. For God has joined both together, and no man may put them apart.

3\. That we aim at our own good as a reward that follows the duty God commands, and so are as willing to do the duty as we are eager for the reward.

4\. That our own benefit is not the #emph[only] thing, no, nor the #emph[chief] thing, we aim at in doing our duty. Rather, it should come in as a motive to add an edge to other motives of greater weight and sharpen them. And this is what the order the apostle keeps in setting down his reasons points out to us. For the three former reasons concern God, and the good conscience children ought to keep toward him. The #emph[first] points to #emph[God’s image,] which parents carry #emph[(in the Lord).] The #emph[second] sets out the #emph[right] that God has prescribed to children. The #emph[third] declares #emph[God’s charge.] Only this #emph[fourth,] which is the last, concerns the #emph[profit] and benefit of the children themselves.

=== §. 99. #emph[Of preferring what is honorable to what is profitable.]

From the order just described we may further gather that #emph[Equity and a good conscience ought to move us to do our duty more than our own profit and the benefit that comes to us from it.] Suppose such a conflict should arise between these that they could not both stand together. Suppose that for doing what is right, and what God has commanded, our prosperity must be hindered and our life shortened. Then we should so hold to what is right and commanded by God that prosperity, life and all are let go. All the exhortations in Scripture to forsake goods, lands, life and everything else for righteousness’ sake tend to this purpose. This point is so clear #footnote[#emph[Cic. lib.] 2. #emph[de Offic. & Paradox.] 1.] that the heathen saw it by the glimmer of the light of nature they had. For they could say that what is honorable and right is to be preferred before what is advantageous and profitable.

There is no comparison between what is honorable and what is advantageous, between #emph[right] and #emph[profit.] The one is absolutely necessary for attaining eternal salvation; the other gives only a little quiet and contentment in this world. No, if #emph[profit] is without right, it can give no true contentment or quiet at all.

They are unworthy, therefore, of the name of Christians #footnote[Use.] who aim so wholly and only at their outward profit and prosperity that they do not regard what is right and what God has commanded. If by obeying God and doing what is right they may reap some benefit for themselves, they can be content #footnote[#emph[Nihil bonum Scriptura nisi quod honestum asserit.] \[Scripture declares nothing good but what is honorable.\] #emph[Ambr. Offic. lib.] 2. #emph[cap.] 3.] to yield to it. But if not, farewell all #emph[right,] farewell all God’s commandments. Though they think everything that is profitable is good, whether it is right or wrong, God’s word counts nothing good but what is honorable. Such people therefore can look for no blessing from the Lord.

=== §. 100. #emph[Of the meaning of the third verse.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 6:3.]

    #text(style: "italic")[that it may go well with you, and that you may live long on the earth.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] promise mentioned before in general is set down here in particular. The first words #emph[(that it may go well with you)] are not in the Hebrew text #footnote[Exod 20:12.] where the law is first recorded. That is why they are not set in the usual form of the ten commandments that we use. Yet in #footnote[Deut 5:16.] another place, where the law is repeated, they are set down. And the Greek translation commonly called the #emph[Septuagint] (which the church in the apostles’ time probably used) has them expressly in both places. Now this part of the promise #emph[(that it may go well with you)] is placed first to enlarge the other part, concerning #emph[long life,] which is the chief thing intended. This appears from the fact that long life alone is mentioned where the law is first recorded. It shows that the #emph[long life] #footnote[Long life and prosperity joined together.] God promises will not be a life of woe and misery (for then it would be no blessing, but the longer life lasted, the worse it would be), but a life full of comfort and happiness. That is why #emph[Moses] sets this former clause in the latter place, after long life, thus: “so that your days may be long and that it #footnote[Deut 5:16.] may go well with you.” He does this to show that the #emph[well-being] spoken of here enlarges the benefit of #emph[long life.]

The apostle sets down the place where the benefit of this promise is to be enjoyed in the broadest phrase, #emph[(on the earth).] The law brings it into a narrower compass, “in the land that the #smallcaps[Lord] your God is giving you,” meaning the land of Canaan, which God gave to the Jews as their own special inheritance. So the promise, as the law sets it down for the Jews in particular, implies #emph[long life] and #emph[prosperity] in their own inheritance; for to the Jews #emph[long life] out of their own country was counted #emph[no life.] But the apostle, writing to all nations, leaves out that description of Canaan and keeps only the general substance in this phrase #emph[(on the earth).] He sets it down to show that even outward prosperity, and a #emph[long life] in this world, is promised here.

=== §. 101. #emph[Of prosperity: how far it may be a blessing.]

To clear this text further and to apply it better, I will resolve various questions arising out of it, and gather such profitable instructions as it offers.

The promise has two branches.

The first branch #emph[(that it may go well with you)] is very ample #footnote[ἵνα εὖ σοι γένηται.] and large. All good things, all manner of blessings whatever, spiritual and temporal, belonging to soul and body, concerning this life and the life to come, make for a man’s #emph[well-being.] So the first question may be asked:

1\. #emph[Question.] What is the extent of this phrase in this place? #footnote[Temporal prosperity promised. 1 Tim 4:8.]

#emph[Answer.] It may be extended in general to all manner of good things. For “godliness … holds promise for the present life and for the one to come.” But (as I take it) temporal prosperity is chiefly intended here, and that for these reasons.

1\. It is joined with #emph[long life,] which is a temporal blessing.

2\. The last words #emph[(on the earth)] may be referred to this branch of #emph[well-being] as well as to the other, of #emph[long life.]

3\. In the law (from which this clause is taken) it is expressly set down thus: “that it may go well with you in the #footnote[Deut 5:16.] land,” etc.

2\. #emph[Question.] Is outward temporal prosperity, then (such as honor, #footnote[Prosperity a good thing.] health, peace, liberty, goods, etc.), a token of God’s love and favor?

#emph[Answer.] Yes. In itself it is a blessing and a fruit of God’s love, as appears from these reasons.

1\. As it was first made and ordained by God, it is a good thing.

2\. It tends to man’s good if it is rightly used.

3\. It was given to man before he had sinned.

4\. It is promised by God as a reward to those who fear him #footnote[Lev 26:4. #emph[etc.] Deut 28:1. #emph[etc.]] and keep his commandments.

5\. The saints have prayed for it and have been thankful #footnote[Gen 28:20; 32:10, 11.] for it.

6\. Its contrary was first inflicted as a punishment of sin, #footnote[Lev 26:15. #emph[etc.]] and is often threatened as a token of God’s wrath, and so it has also been inflicted on transgressors. #footnote[Deut 28:16. #emph[etc.]]

=== §. 102. #emph[Of prosperity bestowed on the wicked, how it proves a curse.]

3\. #emph[Question.] Why then is it given to the wicked, even to those who are #footnote[θεοστυγεῖς.] haters of God and are hated by him? And why are God’s friends, those who are #footnote[φιλόθεοι.] loved by him and love him in return, deprived of it? This painful doubt made #emph[David] stumble, #footnote[Ps 73:13. Jer 12:1. Hab 1:3.] and moved other prophets to complain. But the answer is ready.

#emph[Answer.] Outward prosperity is of such a nature that it may turn to the good or the harm of the one who enjoys it. And in this God’s wonderful and unsearchable wisdom is seen, in that he is able to turn blessings into curses and curses into blessings. #footnote[How prosperity proves a curse to the wicked.] He can work by contraries.

4\. #emph[Question.] How is prosperity a curse to the wicked?

#emph[Answer.] Purely by consequence, through their abuse of it. God gives it to them to show the riches of his mercy; and so that all may taste of it, he does good to the #emph[evil] and the #emph[good.]#footnote[Matt 5:45. Rom 2:4.] Besides, he tests them in this way, to see whether by any means they may be brought to repentance. Because they do not have that gift, their prosperity proves to be a means of making them the more inexcusable, and of increasing their just condemnation the more. For the more God’s blessings abound toward them, the more they abuse them, adding to all their other sins that most odious sin of ingratitude, which completes the heap. And in these respects I may say of the prosperity of the wicked what the prophet said of their king: God gives it in his anger, and takes it away in #footnote[Hos 13:11.] his wrath. For by their abuse of it, it proves to be Satan’s bait to lure them, his snare to catch them, and his hook to drown #footnote[1 Tim 6:9.] them in ruin and destruction. In a word, then, the wicked are fed in a fair pasture, like oxen appointed for the slaughter. They are raised up high, as on a ladder or scaffold, like thieves and traitors, to be brought down with shame and destruction, as #emph[Pharaoh’s] baker was lifted up. #footnote[Gen 40:19.]

=== §. 103. #emph[How both having and lacking prosperity is a blessing to the saints.]

5\. #emph[Question.] How is the enjoying or the lacking of prosperity a blessing to the righteous?

#emph[Answer.] God in his wisdom knows what is best for them and deals with them accordingly. He gives them prosperity as far as he sees it will turn to their good, and withholds it from them as far as he sees it will turn to their harm. Whenever God gives any temporal blessing to his saints, therefore, it is a token of his favor; and whenever he withholds any, the very withholding is also a fruit of his favor. In this the word is made true that “God works all things together for the good of those #footnote[Rom 8:28.] who love Him.” So if they #emph[abound,] it will go well with them; if they #emph[lack,] it will go well with them; if they are in #emph[high place,] it will go well with them; if in #emph[lowly place,] it will go well with them. Whether they are at liberty or in prison, whether in health or sick, in whatever condition, it will go well with them.

6\. #emph[Question.] How is it, then, that saints are often brought to such extremities that they are forced to complain that it goes very #footnote[The complaints of saints do not call their well-being into question.] badly with them?

#emph[Answer.] There is flesh and blood in them, and because of its weakness they are forced to complain. But the present impression of weak flesh is not enough to call the truth of God’s promise into question. In their present extremity they do not consider what God’s mind is, what his manner of dealing with them is, how needful it is that they should be dealt with so, and what end and outcome the Lord will give. In truth it is better with them than they know. There are some weighty reasons that move God to bring them to the extremity they are in. These concern his #emph[own glory,]#footnote[John 9:3. 2 Cor 12:9.] or the #emph[building up]#footnote[Eph 3:1.] of others, or their own good: as #footnote[Ps 119:67.] curing some dangerous disease, #footnote[Job 1:12.] displaying the grace of God given to them, #footnote[Hos 5:15.] drawing them nearer to God, #footnote[2 Cor 5:2.] making them long the more for heaven, and the like.

=== §. 104. #emph[Of long life: how far it is a blessing.]

Concerning the second branch of God’s promise #emph[(long life),] other questions are to be resolved.

1\. #emph[Question.] Is long life a blessing? #footnote[Long life a blessing.]

#emph[Answer.] Yes. Otherwise God would not have promised it as a reward, here and in other places, nor have given it to his saints.

The reasons proving it to be a blessing may be gathered under three heads: 1. #emph[God’s glory.] 2. #emph[The good of the church] where they live. 3. #emph[Their own good.]

1\. God’s glory is much advanced by the long life of the saints. For the longer they live, the more they themselves observe God’s wonderful works, and the more they make them known and declare them to others. But in the grave all #footnote[Ps 6:5. Isa 38:18.] is forgotten.

2\. God’s church is greatly built up by it. In this respect the apostle says, “it is more necessary for #footnote[Phil 1:24.] you#super[+] that I remain in the body.” In the saints, what #emph[Elihu] says should be is true, namely that “age should speak, and many years should teach wisdom.” #footnote[Job 32:7.] The longer the saints live, the more good they do; but after death they do none: “Night is coming, when no one can work.” #footnote[John 9:4.] #footnote[Gal 6:10.] On this ground the apostle exhorts us to #emph[do good while we have time.]

3\. By living long, the saints gain for themselves great honor and dignity among God’s people, and a strong, steadfast confidence in God. Men value a good old servant; much more will God. Old saints have two strong props to establish them and make them bold. One is the remembrance of God’s former favors, by which their hope of eternal life is made more sure to them. The other is a kind of present expectation of the fulfillment of God’s promises, which they have long waited for.

By this it appears that this particular promise is no light matter of small weight, but a strong motive to stir up children to obedience.

=== §. 105. #emph[Of long life proving a curse to the wicked.]

2\. #emph[Question.] Why then is long life given to many wicked people? And why are many saints cut off?

#emph[Answer.] Long life is of the same kind as prosperity: it may be turned into a curse as well as prove a blessing.

By living long on earth the wicked make their sins grow to the full (as is implied of the Amorites).#footnote[Gen 15:16.] They make their name stink the more on earth, as a carcass stinks the more the longer it remains above ground. And they bring the greater torment in hell on themselves: for as #footnote[Rom 2:5.] sin is increased, so that torment will be increased.

The righteous have their days shortened for their good, when they are shortened, and that in these and similar respects:

+ That they may be #footnote[1 Kgs 14:13.] taken away from the evil to come.#footnote[Isa 57:1.]
+ That they might be made an #footnote[1 Kgs 13:24.] example to others.
+ That #footnote[1 Cor 11:32.] by a temporal death eternal condemnation might be avoided.
+ That their #footnote[Gen 5:24. Heb 11:5.] chief and greatest reward might be hastened.

=== §. 106. #emph[Of limiting the promises of temporal blessings.]

So we see that there may be just cause to alter the latter branch of this promise, #emph[long life,] as well as the former, #emph[prosperity,] and yet no wrong comes to the righteous by it, nor any benefit to the wicked.

3\. #emph[Question.] Is not the truth of the promise called into question by this?

#emph[Answer.] Not in the least. For first, all promises of temporal blessings are limited by a condition such as this: #emph[if keeping it may stand with God’s honor and the good of the person to whom it is made.] 2. God never simply deprives his saints of what is promised, but only gives them something better in its place. In taking away #emph[wealth,] he gives a greater store of #emph[grace;] in restraining #emph[liberty of body,] he gives #emph[freedom of conscience;] with #emph[affliction] he gives #emph[patience;] by taking away this #emph[temporal life,] he gives #emph[eternal life.] In this God deals like one who, having promised so much iron, gives as much silver instead, or gold for silver, and so for one pound gives the value of hundreds or thousands.

=== §. 107. #emph[Of applying prosperity and long life to the obedience that children give their parents.]

4\. #emph[Question.] Why are #emph[long life] and #emph[prosperity] assigned to this kind of righteousness in particular?

#emph[Answer.] They are not so assigned to this as if they belonged to no other. For elsewhere they are promised in general to the #footnote[Deut 6:2.] keepers of the whole law, and to other particular branches of it besides this one. Yet in these and similar particular respects #footnote[Ps 34:12, 13, 14.] they are applied to the obedience of children.

1\. Because obedience to parents is one of the surest evidences of our conformity to the whole law. By it we show our regard for God’s image, and lay a good foundation for performing every duty to man.

2\. Because a child’s performing his duty to his parents is, under God, a special means of their doing well and living long. (For as rebellious children make their parents go to their graves the sooner with grief, so dutiful children make them continue the longer in prosperity.) Therefore the Lord in return promises such a child prosperity and long life.

3\. Because parents are a special means of procuring the welfare and long life of their children. Partly they do it by their #emph[provident care,] as #emph[Naomi] said to #emph[Ruth,] “Should I not seek a resting place for you, #footnote[Ruth 3:1.] that it may be well with you?” And partly they do it by their #emph[earnest prayer:] for the faithful prayer of parents for dutiful children has great force with God. From this the custom has arisen of children asking their parents’ blessing, and of parents blessing their children. In this respect the law sets forth the blessing of the fifth commandment thus: #emph[they will prolong your days.] #footnote[יארכון]

4\. Because disobedience to parents brings much harm on children’s heads, and often shortens their days, and that in many ways.

1\. Parents are often provoked by their children’s disobedience #footnote[How disobedient children hinder their welfare and shorten their days.] to disinherit them, or at least to allow them a smaller portion, so that it does not go so well with them. Indeed, some are provoked to bring their rebellious children before the magistrate, who by God’s law was to cut them off,#footnote[Deut 21:21.] so that their life is shortened by it.

2\. Parents are provoked to complain to God of their children’s disobedience, and God is moved by this both to lay heavy judgments on such children in their lifetime and to shorten their days. For parents’ complaints make a loud cry in God’s ears. It is said that God, by cutting off #emph[Abimelech] with an untimely death,#footnote[Judg 9:56.] “repaid the wickedness that Abimelech had done to his father.”

3\. When parents are too indulgent toward their children, God punishes the sin of both parent and child by shortening the child’s days. Take for example #emph[Hophni]#footnote[1 Sam 2:34.] and #emph[Phinehas, Absalom,]#footnote[2 Sam 18:14.] and #emph[Adonijah.]#footnote[1 Kgs 2:25.]

4\. Disobedience to parents is a sin that seldom goes alone, for an undutiful child is commonly a very wicked person in many other ways. Considering how prone our nature is to every sin, it cannot be avoided that those who shake off the yoke of government at the beginning should run headlong into all wild living, looseness and licentiousness. So with sin added to sin, it must bring harm upon harm, until at length life is cut off.

Since these harms are avoided by giving due obedience to parents, it may well be said that #emph[it will go well with obedient children, and they will live long.]

=== §. 108. #emph[Of God’s ordering his favors so that they may appear to be true blessings.]

Now that the particular branches of God’s promise have been #footnote[How fitly prosperity and long life are joined together.] opened one by one, we will consider them jointly, for they greatly enlarge one another. #emph[Prosperity] sweetens long life and makes it welcome; otherwise to live long, that is, in misery and wretchedness, is very tiresome and grievous. Again, long life added to prosperity makes it so much the greater blessing. For the longer a good thing lasts, the better it is. If prosperity were only like a flower, soon gone, the very thought of how fleeting it is would greatly lessen its joy and comfort. But both of them joined together show that what is promised is no small blessing.

From the joining of them both together, I observe that

#emph[God so orders his favors that they appear to be true blessings,] #footnote[God makes his favors true blessings. Gen 17:19.] tending indeed to the good of those on whom he bestows them. So when God gave #emph[Abraham] a son, he established his covenant with him, that this gift might be a true blessing.

I might give the same instance in all the children of promise, such as #emph[Samson, Solomon, John the Baptist, etc.] So in other favors. When God added fifteen years to #emph[Hezekiah’s] life, he also promised #footnote[Isa 38:5, 6; 39:8.] him deliverance from his enemies, and peace and truth all his days. And when God gave #emph[David] a kingdom, he gave him #footnote[2 Sam 7:9. #emph[etc.]] great victories and long life, and established his kingdom for his descendants; and he dealt in the same way with #emph[Solomon.] But not to dwell on any more particulars, this doctrine is excellently confirmed in #emph[Psalm] 128.

In this way God will show that he gives in love even the temporal blessings he gives to his saints, so that they may value them accordingly, and that their hearts may be the more enlarged both to wonder at his goodness and to be thankful for it.

This is the use we must make of the things the Lord is #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.]] pleased to give us, such as long life, good health, honor, peace, plenty, liberty and all prosperity. We must receive and use them as God gives them, namely, as tokens of his favor, and be the more stirred up by them to perform the duties he requires of us. We must not abuse them to his dishonor and our own harm, but use them rather so that he may have honor and we profit by them.

=== §. 109. #emph[Of God’s high regard for dutiful children.]

More particularly, by this promise we may learn what high #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.]] esteem and great regard God has for dutiful children, and for the obedience they give their parents. This ought all the more to move children to complete obedience, if at least they have any care for God’s favor and the tokens of his love. Oh, consider this, all you#super[+] who have parents to honor! Consider how concerned and how earnest God is, in every way and by all means, to draw you#super[+] to obedience. He does not content himself with urging the justice of the point, the place of your#super[+] parent, and the charge he himself has given. He presses your#super[+] own profit most of all, and that not only in hope for the time to come, but in present enjoyment in this life. And he does so because we, in our childishness, are moved most by things that are present and that we can see and feel. He deals with us as a tender father who provides for his child not only a good calling and a fair inheritance, but also gives him plums, pears and such things as delight him for the present, the more to draw him on.

=== §. 110. #emph[Of children’s doing good to themselves by honoring their parents.]

Children may further learn from this promise that in #footnote[3\. #emph[Use.]] performing their duty they do good not only to their parents, but also to themselves: they procure their own welfare and long life. What utter fools, then, disobedient children are! They regard neither God, nor their parents, nor themselves. They deprive themselves of their eternal happiness, hinder their welfare and shorten their days. So I may fitly apply to undutiful children these words of the psalmist: “Mark the obedient child, for the end of him is peace; #footnote[Ps 37:37, 38.] but the rebellious will be destroyed; he will be cut off.” And these of the wise man: “I know that it will go well with the dutiful #footnote[Eccl 8:12, 13.] child, but it will not go well with the disobedient; he will not prolong his days.” And these of the prophet: “Tell the obedient child that it will be well with him;#footnote[Isa 3:10, 11.] he will eat the fruit of his doings; but woe to the transgressor; it will be ill with him.”

=== §. 111. #emph[Of parents doing good to their children by keeping them under obedience.]

From this promise parents may learn how to do good #footnote[4\. #emph[Use.]] to their children, how to provide for their welfare and to preserve their life long on earth (a thing most parents are naturally inclined to, and greatly desire). It is done by teaching children their duty and keeping them under obedience. Then they have God’s promise to assure them that it will go well with their children and that they will live long. When parents are on their deathbeds, they may rest more securely on this promise than on a great store of treasure laid up for their children, or great revenues reserved for them. Many parents neglect themselves. They toil and drudge, they fret and worry, they pinch and save, to leave their children a store of wealth, thinking by this to do good to their children. Yet at the same time they pamper their children too much, give them free rein, and do not care how little duty they perform. God’s curse will lie on all the store laid up for such children, like a fire to consume it all. Does not daily experience prove the truth of this? The judgments laid on some such children plainly show God’s just indignation against them all. So let rich men not think they have left their children well provided for if they leave them a large portion, but rather if they have seen to it that they are obedient children. And if poor men’s children are such, let them not fear: it will go well with them.

It is said that a good trade is better than house and land; but by virtue of this promise we may say that obedience in a child is better than trade and all. This is the #emph[trade of a child’s] #footnote[Prov 22:6.] #emph[way] that parents should teach their children. So as parents desire their children’s good, they ought to be wise in procuring it, and that is by teaching them this #emph[trade of obedience.] So they will bring much comfort to themselves while they live, and good to their children after them.

=== §. 112. #emph[Of the lasting substance of things whose circumstances concerning the Jews have passed away.]

In laying down this particular promise, the apostle puts in place of its limitation to the Jews, in these words, “in the land that the #smallcaps[Lord] your God is giving you,” a general phrase that extends it to all nations, namely this, #emph[(on the earth).] From this I gather that

#emph[The substance of those things that in some circumstances were] #footnote[#emph[Doctrine.] The substance of promises made to the Jews still remains.] #emph[restricted to the Jews in a special manner remains in force for all Christians.] The substance of this promise was that it should go well with obedient children while they lived here on earth, and that in this welfare they should live long. The circumstance was that they should enjoy that blessing in Canaan. Though Christians do not live in Canaan, which is the circumstance, yet it will go well with them, and they will live long, which is the substance. So too, though the circumstance of God’s covenant with #emph[Abraham] (which was #emph[circumcision)] is abolished, yet the substance (which is that he will be #emph[our God, and the God of our descendants)] remains. This might be illustrated further by many hundreds of instances. For the substance of all the Jewish sacrifices and sacraments, both ordinary and extraordinary, of their sabbaths, their fasts, their feasts and the like, remains, though the circumstances, like shadows, have vanished away. That is why many promises made to them are applied by the apostles to Christians, such as this: “Never will I leave you, #footnote[Heb 13:5. Acts 2:39.] never will I forsake you.” And in general it is said, “This promise belongs to you#super[+] and your#super[+] children and to all who are far off.”

By this we may learn what use to make of the Old Testament, #footnote[Use.] even of those promises and privileges that in some particular respects were assigned to the Jews: namely, by observing the substance and distinguishing it from the circumstance. In this way we will find true what the apostle says of all things written in former times, namely, that they were “written for our instruction.”#footnote[Rom 15:4.] In this respect the same apostle says of the things recorded of #emph[Abraham] that they “were written not only for Abraham,”#footnote[Rom 4:23.] and again of the things recorded of the Israelites, that they “were written down as warnings for us.” #footnote[1 Cor 10:11.] By this we may learn how to apply the preface to the ten commandments, which mentions the deliverance of Israel out of the bondage of Egypt.

Pray, therefore, for the spirit of illumination to discern between #emph[substance] and #emph[circumstance,] especially in reading the Old Testament.

=== §. 113. #emph[Of the appointed term of man’s life.]

Having declared the orthodox points this text affords, I will further point out two heretical positions that our adversaries raise from it. One is held by those who call themselves #emph[Lutherans,] to the dishonor of the man whom God raised up to be a worthy instrument in dispelling the mist of popery, which had greatly darkened the light of the gospel. The other is held by the #emph[papists.]

The former is this: #emph[God has not determined the set limit of] #footnote[Hinc patescit humanae vitae terminum, non esse decreto simplici & absoluto constitutum, etc. Zach. Muthel. in hunc loc. & N. Heming. alijque. \[From this it is plain that the limit of human life is not fixed by a simple and absolute decree, etc. Zach. Muthel. on this place, and N. Hemming and others.\]] #emph[man’s days,] but it is in man’s power to lengthen or shorten them. For if it were otherwise, they say, this promise of long life and others like it would be to no purpose, and so would the contrary threats of shortening man’s days.

To answer this fully, I will first show that the position itself is directly contrary to the whole current of Scripture, and then expose the unsoundness of their inference.

Concerning the determined limit of man’s days, Scripture speaks thus: “Is there not an appointed time for man on #footnote[Man’s time set. Job 7:1.] earth? Are not his days like those of a hired hand?” Note with what emphasis the point is set forth, as if it were so clear that no one could doubt it. Note also the two metaphors used here, which greatly clear the point: one taken from soldiers, the other from hired servants. The one from soldiers is implied in the meaning of the original word translated #emph[appointed time,] which properly means one who has #footnote[צבא] his time appointed for warfare, or the time itself so appointed; the other is expressed. Now we know that these times are appointed to the hour; so is the time of man’s life. In this respect #emph[Job] says again, “All the days of my appointed time I will #footnote[Job 14:14.] wait,” etc., where he uses the same word as before, in the same sense. To this purpose such phrases as these are often used in Scripture: “determined days,” “the number of days,” #footnote[Job 14:5. Eccl 2:3. John 7:30.] “hour,” etc. Did not the prophet expressly declare to #emph[Hezekiah] that he would live exactly fifteen years after his sickness?#footnote[Isa 38:5.] He could not have told it if the Lord had not set that limit beforehand. Christ says that “the very hairs of your#super[+] head are all numbered”;#footnote[Matt 10:30; 6:27.] are not our days much more numbered? Again he says, “Who … can add one cubit to his stature?” #footnote[#emph[Stat sua cuique dies, etc.] \[Each man’s day stands fixed, etc.\] #emph[Virg. Aeneid.] 10.] Can anyone, then, add to his days? This point is so evident that the heathen noted it.

As for their inference #emph[(if a man’s time is determined, all the promises of long life are to no purpose),] I answer that God, who has set down the exact time and limit of man’s life, has also set down the means of reaching that limit. Now the time he has kept secret to himself; the means he has revealed to us. With regard to us, then, who do not know the time appointed by the Lord, it may be said that by using such and such means we prolong our days, or by doing such and such things we shorten them. Now because these means only show our days to be long or short, God’s decree remains firm and stable and is not altered by them. Yet this work of lengthening or shortening is attributed to us, because we do what lies in us toward it, and do it freely, without any compulsion. For though God’s decree causes a necessity in the outcome, it imposes no constraint on the will of man, but leaves it as free (with regard to the manner of working) as if there were no decree at all. And in this God’s wonderful wisdom is displayed: that notwithstanding his determined purpose in things, man has no ground for an excuse to say he was forced to this or that.

The knowledge of this determined limit of man’s life is of great use, for it teaches us:

1\. To submit ourselves wholly to God, and to be prepared #footnote[Uses to be made of the determined time of man’s life.] either to depart out of this world soon or to live in it long, as God orders our time; neither desiring to live longer than God has appointed, nor grieving to live as long as he has appointed.

2\. Not to fear the threats of any man, so as to be drawn away #footnote[Dan 3:17, 18.] from God by them.

3\. To do God’s work while we have time,#footnote[John 9:4.] etc.

=== §. 114. #emph[Of the reward promised to obedience, and that it implies no merit.]

The other heresy, which the papists gather from this text, is #footnote[Against merit.] this: #emph[Man’s obedience is meritorious.]

#emph[Answer.] The reward promised here is no matter of wages and what is deserved, but of pure grace and favor.

I have spoken more fully of this error #footnote[#emph[Whole Armour of God,] Treatise 2, Part 4, §. 7, on Eph 6:14.] elsewhere.
